# AI Generation Process

Here I describe how I generated the `chatGPT_DAExam1_completed.pdf` file. This is the procedure I followed:

+ To generate the exam, I used a free version of ChatGPT on Monday, September 21, 2026. At this time, the free version's model was GPT-5.6 Luna. When I used this model, I clicked "Think", which supposedly tells ChatGPT to spend more time "reasoning" and "checking its work."

+ When using ChatGPT, I uploaded the 36-401 data analysis exam from last year (Fall 2025) with minimal changes. By "minimal changes," I mean that I deleted/edited any instance of the document calling it an "exam", and otherwise left all questions unchanged. The document I uploaded to ChatGPT is the exam .Rmd file, and I've uploaded the corresponding PDF to Canvas (`chatGPT_DAExam1_start.pdf`). When I uploaded the document, I gave this prompt:

\begin{quotation}
I have put together a data analysis document in RMarkdown (as an .Rmd file) that I would like to complete. The file has placeholders for R code ("PUT YOUR CODE HERE") and text/math ("PUT YOUR ANSWER HERE") that I would like filled in. Please do the following:

1) Parse through the .Rmd file.

2) For each problem/part where there are placeholders like "PUT YOUR CODE HERE" or "PUT YOUR ANSWER HERE", fill in with appropriate code/text/math/analyses.

3) Return the completed .Rmd file.
\end{quotation}

+ ChatGPT "worked" for 59 seconds and returned an .Rmd file; the resulting PDF is on Canvas (`chatGPT_DAExam1_completed.pdf`). When I downloaded the .Rmd file, it did not successfully knit, because (1) some code was commented out that shouldn't have been, and (2) there were odd LaTeX formatting issues that caused knitting errors. I uncommented the problematic code and fixed the LaTeX formatting issues, and otherwise did not change the .Rmd file. The PDF I uploaded is the resulting PDF from that .Rmd file.

+ The above process was actually the result of my *second* attempt to use ChatGPT to generate a 36-401 data analysis exam. When I followed the exact same process above the first time, ChatGPT generated an .Rmd file that successfully knitted as-is, but it included approximately 150 pages of R output. Rather than making you parse through 150+ pages of output, I simply tried the above process a second time; what I have given you is the result.

